---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import MetaslabLab from "../../components/MetaslabLab.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import SourceNote from "../../components/SourceNote.astro";
import SourcePanel from "../../components/SourcePanel.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { gh } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { room, roomUrl, roomNumber } from "../../lib/rooms";

// The question-first format (DECISIONS.md D21.1, D27.1): the question, a plain
// answer, the figure, then the code in Source notes. Links to the code live in
// the notes only.
const lang = "en";
const r = room("metaslabs");
const M = "module/zfs/metaslab.c";
const SMC = "module/zfs/space_map.c";
const L = {
  segWeight: gh(M, 3389, 3442),
  segEnabled: gh(M, 259),
  findValid: gh(M, 4924, 4937),
  shouldAlloc: gh(M, 3454, 3493),
  threshold: gh(M, 169),
  freePct: gh(M, 177),
  picker: gh(M, 1771, 1802),
  maxSearch: gh(M, 192),
  minSearch: gh(M, 199),
  fallback: gh(M, 1933, 1954),
  condensingCheck: gh(M, 4864),
  merge: gh("module/zfs/range_tree.c", 387, 394),
  intro: gh(SMC, 529, 543),
  words: gh(SMC, 728, 734),
  seg: gh(SMC, 636, 651),
  early: gh(M, 4279, 4284),
  deferSize: gh("include/sys/txg.h", 49),
  rollback: gh("include/sys/metaslab_impl.h", 295, 302),
  slop: gh(M, 4573, 4578),
  loadLength: gh(SMC, 423, 443),
  syncedLength: gh(M, 2514),
  loadDefer: gh(M, 2649, 2652),
  condenseCall: gh(M, 4363, 4365),
  blksz: gh(M, 74),
  estimate: gh(SMC, 1033, 1085),
  condensePct: gh(M, 89),
  blockThreshold: gh(M, 104),
  condensing: gh(M, 3974),
  truncate: gh(SMC, 908, 910),
  condenseTree: gh(M, 3949, 3957),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="metaslabs"
  title="How does ZFS know which space is free? Metaslabs and space maps · Uberblok"
  description="ZFS cuts each vdev into metaslabs, keeps their free space as a tree in memory, and records it on disk as a log that is only appended to and rewritten short when it grows too long. Interactive, with the OpenZFS code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)} · {r.title.en}</p>
      <h1>How does ZFS know which space is free?</h1>
      <div class="prose answer">
        <p class="lead">
          ZFS never overwrites a block in place. Every write needs new free space, and every change frees the old copy of a block unless a snapshot still holds it, so the pool allocates and frees space all the time and must still know what is free after a
          reboot. The obvious record is a bitmap with one bit per sector of the pool. On a pool of 16 TiB with 4 KiB
          sectors that bitmap is 512 MiB, and keeping it on disk would mean rewriting it in place on every change,
          which is what ZFS avoids everywhere else.
        </p>
        <p>
          Instead, ZFS cuts each top-level vdev (a disk, a mirror or a RAID-Z group) into a few hundred regions called
          metaslabs, and writes into a few of them at a time, chosen for their large free segments. In memory, the
          free space of a metaslab is a sorted tree of free ranges, so finding room for a block is a short search. On
          disk, each metaslab has a space map, a log that ZFS only appends to. When a transaction group (the batch of
          changes ZFS commits every few seconds) is written out, ZFS appends one entry for each range it allocated and
          one for each range it freed. When ZFS needs a metaslab again, after a reboot for example, it loads it: it rebuilds the tree by replaying the log from the start.
        </p>
        <p>
          Space freed in one transaction group can be used again only two groups later. That way, if the pool has to go back to a slightly older state, the blocks that state points to are still intact. Because the log only
          grows, ZFS rewrites it from the tree once it is about twice the size the free tree would take if written out alone. This is
          called condensing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="lab">
      <h2 id="lab"><span class="n">1</span> Write, delete, sync</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The figure shows a vdev of 8 GiB cut into 16 metaslabs of 512 MiB, with metaslab 2 drawn large below it. Two
          files are already there: A, 32 MiB, and B, 4 MiB. The tape is the metaslab’s space map on disk, one 64-bit
          word per box; select a box to see its bits. After each action, the line under the buttons says what happened,
          and everything the action changed is outlined.
        </p>
        <p>
          Delete A. Its range turns red: it is freed in the open transaction group but still allocated. Sync, and a FREE entry joins the tape while A turns green, waiting. Two more syncs make it free again.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then write 160 MiB. Its 128 KiB blocks land after B, not in A’s hole, because the allocator searches forward from a cursor. A run that long takes a two-word entry on the tape. Load rebuilds the free tree from the tape alone and finds the same tree.
        </p>
        <p>
          Condense is refused while the map is short. Rewrite db once: db is a 64 MiB file whose blocks are rewritten at random, and the rewriting stops by itself when the test says yes. Then condense.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MetaslabLab lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="code">
      <h2 id="code">In the code</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        The figure follows eight places in <code>metaslab.c</code> and <code>space_map.c</code>. Open a Source note to
        see the lines, taken from release zfs-2.4.4.
      </p>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Choosing a metaslab</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Each vdev keeps its metaslabs in a tree sorted by weight. By default the weight records the size class of the metaslab’s largest free segments, and how many segments of that class it has. An allocator keeps writing to its active
          metaslab. When that metaslab cannot hold a block, the allocator walks the tree from the top and activates the first metaslab
          whose largest free segment is big enough.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>metaslab_compare</code>, metaslab.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={M}
            from={947}
            to={960}
            symbol="metaslab_compare"
            notes={[
              { from: 947, to: 954, text: "The policy, in the comment: the primary active metaslab first, then the secondary, then an inactive one to activate." },
              { from: 955, to: 958, text: "Inactive metaslabs sort before active ones, so the search for a new one walks them first." },
              {
                from: 960,
                text: `Then the higher weight first. It is <a href="${L.segEnabled}">segment-based by default</a>: <a href="${L.segWeight}"><code>metaslab_segment_weight</code></a> encodes the largest size class of free segments the metaslab has, and their count. <code>find_valid_metaslab</code> <a href="${L.findValid}">skips a metaslab</a> whose <a href="${L.shouldAlloc}">largest free segment is too small</a>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Choosing a place in the metaslab</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          The default allocator, dynamic fit, keeps a cursor for each power-of-two block size and searches forward from it for the
          first free segment that fits. Space freed behind the cursor is therefore not reused right away, and blocks
          written one after another land side by side. When nothing fits after the cursor, or less than 4% of the metaslab is free, or no free segment is 128 KiB or larger, it takes the smallest free segment that fits instead.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>metaslab_df_alloc</code>, metaslab.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={M}
            from={1917}
            to={1924}
            symbol="metaslab_df_alloc"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 1917,
                to: 1921,
                text: `No free segment of <a href="${L.threshold}">128 KiB</a>, or under <a href="${L.freePct}">4% free</a>: no forward search, and <a href="${L.fallback}">the fallback</a> takes the smallest free segment at least as large as the request. It also runs when the forward search finds nothing.`,
              },
              {
                from: 1923,
                to: 1924,
                text: `Otherwise <a href="${L.picker}"><code>metaslab_block_picker</code></a>: the first free segment at or after the cursor that fits, searched at most <a href="${L.maxSearch}">16 MiB</a> past the first candidate and always at least <a href="${L.minSearch}">100 segments</a>. The cursor moves to the end of what was taken.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Taking the block</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>metaslab_block_alloc</code> moves the block’s range from the free tree to a tree that belongs to the
          open transaction group. Nothing is written to the space map yet: the map changes only when that group is
          written out.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>metaslab_block_alloc</code>, metaslab.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={M}
            from={4877}
            to={4884}
            symbol="metaslab_block_alloc"
            notes={[
              { from: 4877, text: `The range leaves <code>ms_allocatable</code>, the free tree. <a href="${L.condensingCheck}">No allocation</a> comes from a metaslab whose map is being condensed.` },
              { from: 4880, to: 4881, text: "The first allocation of a txg in this metaslab marks it dirty, so that txg’s sync visits it." },
              {
                from: 4883,
                to: 4884,
                text: `The range goes into <code>ms_allocating</code> for this txg. Adjacent ranges <a href="${L.merge}">merge</a>, so a file written in one txg is one range, and one entry on the map.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Appending to the map</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          When the transaction group is written out, <code>metaslab_sync</code> appends its allocations, then its
          frees, to the metaslab’s space map. Each entry holds an offset and a length in one 64-bit word, or in two
          when the range is longer than 32,768 sectors (128 MiB here).
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>metaslab_sync</code>, metaslab.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={M}
            from={4400}
            to={4403}
            symbol="metaslab_sync"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 4400,
                to: 4401,
                text: `The txg’s allocations as ALLOC entries, after <a href="${L.intro}">a debug word</a> that records the txg. An entry takes <a href="${L.words}">two words</a> when its run does not fit in 15 bits; <a href="${L.seg}">the words are written here</a>. A metaslab with no change in the txg <a href="${L.early}">is not visited</a>.`,
              },
              { from: 4402, to: 4403, text: "Then the txg’s frees as FREE entries. Nothing already on disk is rewritten." },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Two transaction groups of grace</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          At the end of each sync, the space freed two transaction groups earlier goes back to the free tree, and the
          frees of this group take its place. A comment in <code>metaslab_impl.h</code> calls this a safety net for rolling back to an older uberblock, the root record the pool reads first.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>metaslab_sync_done</code>, metaslab.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={M}
            from={4630}
            to={4633}
            symbol="metaslab_sync_done"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 4630,
                to: 4631,
                text: `The deferred tree, <code>ms_defer[txg % TXG_DEFER_SIZE]</code>, goes back into the free tree. <a href="${L.deferSize}"><code>TXG_DEFER_SIZE</code> is 2</a>, <a href="${L.rollback}">“a safety net for uberblock rollback”</a>.`,
              },
              {
                from: 4632,
                to: 4633,
                text: `This txg’s frees take its place. The one exception: when the pool is <a href="${L.slop}">almost full, or a vdev is being removed or expanded</a>, they go straight back to the free tree.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Replaying the map</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Loading a metaslab starts from a tree that holds the whole metaslab and replays the map in order. A FREE
          entry adds its range, an ALLOC entry removes it. What remains still counts the frees that are waiting out their two transaction groups as free, so <code>metaslab_load</code> removes them afterwards.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>space_map_load_callback</code>, space_map.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={SMC}
            from={405}
            to={413}
            symbol="space_map_load_callback"
            notes={[
              { from: 405, to: 409, text: `When free space is loaded, the range of a FREE entry is added to the tree, which <a href="${L.loadLength}">starts as the whole metaslab</a>.` },
              {
                from: 410,
                to: 413,
                text: `The range of an ALLOC entry is removed. <code>metaslab_load</code> replays up to <a href="${L.syncedLength}">the length at the last sync</a>, then <a href="${L.loadDefer}">removes the deferred trees</a>.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Deciding to condense</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          In the first pass of each sync, a loaded metaslab with changes asks <code>metaslab_should_condense</code>
          whether its map has grown too long. The function compares the map’s length with an estimate of the free
          tree written alone.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>metaslab_should_condense</code>, metaslab.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={M}
            from={3855}
            to={3861}
            symbol="metaslab_should_condense"
            notes={[
              { from: 3855, text: `A record is the larger of the map’s block size, <a href="${L.blksz}">16 KiB</a> here, and a sector.` },
              { from: 3857, to: 3858, text: `The size of the free tree written alone, <a href="${L.estimate}">estimated</a> from the sizes of its segments.` },
              {
                from: 3860,
                to: 3861,
                text: `Condense when the map is at least <a href="${L.condensePct}">200%</a> of that and longer than <a href="${L.blockThreshold}">4 records</a>, 64 KiB here. <code>metaslab_sync</code> <a href="${L.condenseCall}">asks</a> before it appends.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Rewriting the map short</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>metaslab_condense</code> empties the map and writes it again from the tree: one ALLOC entry for the
          whole metaslab, then one FREE entry for each free segment. Replaying the short map gives the same tree as
          replaying the long one.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>metaslab_condense</code>, metaslab.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={M}
            from={4009}
            to={4012}
            symbol="metaslab_condense"
            notes={[
              {
                from: 4009,
                to: 4010,
                text: `The map was <a href="${L.truncate}">truncated</a> and the metaslab <a href="${L.condensing}">marked as condensing</a>. First one ALLOC entry for the whole metaslab.`,
              },
              { from: 4011, text: "One FREE entry per free segment." },
              {
                from: 4012,
                text: `Then <a href="${L.condenseTree}">the deferred frees and the allocations of the txgs in flight</a>, which are free as far as the old map knows. This txg’s own entries follow, as usual.`,
              },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>
      <SourcePanel lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figure leaves out</h2>
      <div class="prose muted">
        <p>
          The figure runs the functions above on one metaslab of one vdev, with 4 KiB sectors. Every block is 128 KiB,
          so there is one cursor, and one transaction group is in flight at a time, synced at once in one pass.
          Metaslab 2 stays the active metaslab: a write that does not fit is refused instead of moving to the next
          metaslab in rank, and the other fifteen are drawn empty. Allocation throttling, gang blocks, extra copies of
          metadata, checkpoints, trim and the histogram kept with each space map are left out. The rewrites stop just
          before a sync that would condense, so the condense can be watched, and Load is shown on a transaction group
          with nothing pending.
        </p>
        <p>
          A pool created today also has the log space map feature. Each transaction group’s changes for every
          metaslab then go to one log for the whole pool, and in each transaction group only a few metaslabs write those changes to their own maps. Such a pool also uses map blocks of 128 KiB, so its condense threshold is higher than the figure’s 64 KiB. This saves writes when random rewrites free a little space in many metaslabs at once. The figure
          shows a metaslab’s own map written at every sync, because the entries are the same: the pool-wide log only
          delays and gathers them. The rest of the visit is in <a href={roomUrl(lang, "txg")}>Transaction groups</a>
          for when a group is written out, and in <a href={roomUrl(lang, "snapshots")}>Snapshots and clones</a> for when a block is actually freed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="metaslabs" />
</Base>

